  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Dry, breezy conditions and below normal\ntemperatures are expected for today and Friday. \n\nThe upper trough axis that has lingered to our west for several\ndays now will finally progress off to our east today. Dry\nconditions are expected locally, but winds will remain gusty\nout of the north as high pressure remains in place to our north\nand a coastal low starts to develop offshore. There will be\nenough remnant low-level moisture around for a stratocumulus \ndeck to form in response to daytime heating. Temperatures will \nrun well below normal, with highs in the 60s at lower \nelevations, and 50s in the mountains.\n\nSkies are expected to clear out tonight as drier air works in\nfrom the north. The pressure gradient may weaken just enough \ntonight for some of the more sheltered valleys in the \nAlleghenies to decouple. If this occurs, some of the highest\nmountain valleys may get a little frost, with temperatures\ndropping back into the 30s. Current thinking is that coverage of\nthe frost will be low enough to preclude the issuance of a \nFrost Advisory for those locations, but we'll continue to \nmonitor the situation. Further east, we'll likely maintain \nenough of a pressure gradient for winds to persist. Overnight \nlows are generally forecast to be in the 40s to the west of \nI-95, and lower 50s to the east of I-95.\n\nSunnier skies are forecast tomorrow, along with slightly warmer\ntemperatures. Winds will remain gusty out of the north (gusts to\naround 20-25 mph possible), and temperatures are forecast to\nreach into the lower 70s at lower elevations, with 60s in the\nmountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Breezy and warmer this weekend, but forecast \nremains uncertain as Nor'easter impacts Northeast U.S.\n\n\nAnomalously strong surface ridge (by late September standards) \nfinally weakens this weekend as a strong Nor'easter develops off the \nEast Coast. Ensemble members still show quite a bit of spread \nregarding the exact track of the Nor'easter. A few members show \nthe possibility the low tracks closer to the NJ / Delmarva coast\nthis weekend. Regardless of exact position, we are likely to be\non the drier (subsidence) side of the system with northwest \nwinds bringing in breezy, warmer, and mostly dry conditions. If \nthe Nor'easter tracks closer to our area that could bring some \nrain chances to the area, especially along/east of I-95, along \nwith the potential for increasing winds. Safe to say that there \nisn't a definite trend for this weekend, so be sure to keep \ntrack of the forecast for any changes in the coming days.\n\nThe Nor'easter is eventually pushed offshore at the start of next \nweek as high pressure builds in from the OH Valley and Great Lakes. \nMid-level ridging builds over much of the eastern CONUS during the \nmiddle of next week, marking the return of much warmer temperatures \nand continued dry conditions.",
